      body: `# Systematic Debugging

Bugs die to METHOD, not luck. The loop: **Reproduce \u2192 Isolate \u2192 Hypothesize \u2192 Instrument \u2192 Test hypothesis \u2192 Fix \u2192 Verify \u2192 Prevent**. Skipping steps produces symptom patches that come back.

## Step 1 \u2014 Reproduce
Get the EXACT failing case: the precise input, the precise command, the precise environment. If you cannot reproduce it, your first bug is "make it reproducible" (shrink the input, freeze the random, pin the time). A repro you can trigger at will is half the fix.

## Step 2 \u2014 Read the actual error
The full message, not the first line: exception TYPE, MESSAGE, and the deepest STACK FRAME IN YOUR OWN CODE. Translate it: "TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'length') at parseHeader" = "parseHeader received undefined where it expected a string \u2014 find who called it with what". Never guess past an unread error.

## Step 3 \u2014 Isolate
- Shrink the failing case to the smallest input that still fails (delete halves; the bug survives or dies \u2014 either answer is information).
- Draw the boundary: same input, slightly changed \u2192 does it fail? Find exactly WHICH dimension triggers it (value, type, order, size, state, time).
- In a project: is it the module's logic, or the wiring? Test the unit directly (\`run_js\` with the module's function + the bad input) vs through the entry (\`run_project\`).

## Step 4 \u2014 Hypotheses (write them, rank them)
List 2-4 candidate causes with the reasoning: "H1: the regex has no global flag so only first match replaced \u2014 fits because multi-line inputs fail. H2: encoding of Persian text breaks splitting \u2014 fits less because ASCII also fails." Rank by likelihood \xD7 cheapness to check. Check ONE at a time \u2014 changing two things teaches you nothing about either.

## Step 5 \u2014 Instrument
Make the invisible visible: \`print()\` the suspicious values at the boundaries (input as received, intermediate states, branch taken, final value). One targeted print beats twenty speculative edits. In workspace projects, run with \`run_project\` and read the logs. Compare "what the code actually did" vs "what I believed it did" \u2014 the bug lives in that gap.

## Step 6 \u2014 Fix the CAUSE
The fix should make you slightly surprised it was ever broken. If the fix feels like a fight (try/catch around it, coalescing to a default, adding a null check three layers up), you patched a symptom \u2014 go one level deeper and fix where the invariant actually broke (who produced the undefined? why?).

## Step 7 \u2014 Verify BOTH directions
- The failing case now passes. 
- Cases that WORKED still work (regression check \u2014 run the full \`run_tests\`).
- Add a regression test for the exact bug (see **test-engineering**): \`test('handles the 2026-11-07 crash case', \u2026)\`.

## Step 8 \u2014 Prevent (senior move)
Ask: what allowed this bug to exist? Missing validation at a boundary \u2192 add an assert/fail-fast there. Untested branch \u2192 test it. Confusing API \u2192 rename/restructure so the misuse becomes hard. Mention the systemic fix in your answer even when the local one suffices.

## Anti-patterns (career-limiting)
Random edits until it "works" \xB7 fixing without reproducing \xB7 catch-and-continue swallowing the evidence \xB7 blaming the framework/compiler (it's you, 99 times of 100) \xB7 "works on my machine" without stating the environment \xB7 claiming a fix without showing the before/after output in the transcript.`
