      body: `# Data Analysis Method

Analysis answers a QUESTION \u2014 never "look at this data". If the user gave data without a question, the first deliverable is the question set: what decisions could this data inform?

## Step 1 \u2014 Audit the data (before any statistics)
Load it with \`run_js\` (JSON/CSV \u2192 parse, or paste small data inline). Answer:
- Shape: how many rows/columns, what does ONE row mean (the unit of analysis)?
- Each column: type (number/category/date/free text), range, distribution shape.
- **Missingness**: which fields are empty/null, is it random or systematic (rows from one source all missing one field = biased source)?
- Duplicates (exact rows, repeated IDs), impossible values (negative ages, dates in the future, category typos), units (kg vs lb, tomans vs rials \u2014 Persian data: always confirm the currency unit!).

## Step 2 \u2014 Clean, and LOG every decision
Documented transformations only: dropped rows (how many, why), imputed values (method), standardized categories. Never silently "fix" data \u2014 the log IS part of the analysis. Keep the raw copy immutable in a variable.

## Step 3 \u2014 Describe (sanity statistics)
Count, sum, mean/median (report BOTH \u2014 divergence means skew), min/max, quartiles. Sanity-check against reality: totals that should match known figures, percentages that must sum to 100, counts vs row counts. Every sanity failure is a finding, not an inconvenience.

## Step 4 \u2014 Relate (the actual question)
Group comparisons (by category), trends (by time bucket \u2014 state the bucket size and why), correlations (with the mandatory caveat: association \u2260 causation), rankings (state the metric precisely), outliers (investigate, don't delete: the outlier is often THE story).

## Step 5 \u2014 Chart correctly (when a chart earns its place)
Match chart to question: **trend over time \u2192 line**; **comparison across categories \u2192 bar** (horizontal when labels are long); **part-of-whole \u2192 stacked bar or donut (\u22645 slices)**; **distribution \u2192 histogram/box**; **relationship \u2192 scatter**. Never: 3D, dual axes without warning, truncated y-axis on bar charts (bars start at zero), pie with 12 slices. Persian labels, Persian digits, title + axis labels + unit on every chart. Prefer a table when exact values matter more than shape.

## Step 6 \u2014 Narrate (the deliverable)
- **Lead with the answer**: the 1-3 insights that matter, each with its number ("\u0633\u0641\u0627\u0631\u0634\u200C\u0647\u0627\u06CC \u0627\u0633\u0641\u0646\u062F \u06F3\u06F4\u066A \u0628\u06CC\u0634\u062A\u0631 \u0627\u0632 \u0645\u06CC\u0627\u0646\u06AF\u06CC\u0646 \u0628\u0648\u062F\u0647").
- Evidence: the table/chart that supports each insight.
- **Caveats section, always**: sample size, missing data share, data date, assumptions made in cleaning, and what this data CANNOT tell you (no control group, self-reported, one source).
- Next-step offer: deeper cut, different segmentation, exported cleaned dataset (\`create_file\` + \`send_file\`).

## Compute discipline
All arithmetic through \`run_js\` (never in your head): write the computation, print intermediate tables, cross-check totals two ways (sum of parts vs total). Rounding: keep full precision during computation, round only at presentation. State percentages' denominators ("\u06F2\u06F3\u066A \u0627\u0632 \u0686\u0647\u061F").
For anything above ~50KB, chunk the computation; the sandbox is synchronous and capped \u2014 stream aggregates instead of holding everything when possible.`
